---
import Abbr from '../components/Abbr.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, CONTACT_MAILTO } from '../lib/contact';
import { RETENTION_DAYS } from '../lib/contact-form';
import { PLAUSIBLE_ORIGIN } from '../lib/analytics';
import { formatPostDate } from '../lib/post-date';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../lib/site';
import { THEME_STORAGE_KEY } from '../lib/theme';
import { TEXT_SCALE_KEY } from '../lib/text-scale';
import {
  D1_RESTORE_WINDOW_DAYS,
  WORKER_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS,
} from '../lib/platform-facts';

/*
 * Every claim is asserted by tests/privacy.spec.ts, and "Visit counts" by
 * tests/analytics.spec.ts. Cloudflare's own logging is not claimed, only linked.
 */

/* Set by hand when the policy text changes; a build date would re-date it on every deploy. */
const lastUpdated = '2026-11-08';

const CLOUDFLARE_PRIVACY = 'https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/';
const CLOUDFLARE_NEL =
  'https://developers.cloudflare.com/network-error-logging/';
const NEL_REPORT_HOST = 'a.nel.cloudflare.com';
const PLAUSIBLE_DATA_POLICY = 'https://plausible.io/data-policy';
const PLAUSIBLE_TRIAL = 'https://plausible.io/docs/trial';
const PLAUSIBLE_HOST = new URL(PLAUSIBLE_ORIGIN).host;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="My website sets no cookies, loads nothing from anywhere else, and counts visits with cookie-free analytics."
>
  <PageHero title="Privacy" measure="reading" slab="none">
    <p class="standfirst">
      I am Sinduri Guntupalli, and I decide what this website collects. It sets
      no cookies and loads nothing from any other website. Visits are counted
      with Plausible, which does not use cookies either. When you use the
      contact form, I store what you type so I can reply. The request for each
      form submission is logged for {WORKER_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS} days, including
      your <Abbr short="IP" long="Internet Protocol" /> address, the internet
      address of your connection.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-6 text-body">
      Last updated{' '}
      <time datetime={lastUpdated}>
        {formatPostDate(new Date(lastUpdated))}
      </time>
      .
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="reading-layout band py-section">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-page">
      <div class="prose mx-auto max-w-measure">
        <h2>What My Website Does Not Do</h2>

        <ul role="list">
          <li>
            <strong>No cookies.</strong> Nothing on {SITE_NAME} sets a cookie,
            and nothing reads one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Two browser settings.</strong> When you choose Light or Dark
            in the Theme menu, your choice is kept in <dfn>local storage</dfn>,
            a small store of settings in your browser. It is kept under the name{' '}
            <code>{THEME_STORAGE_KEY}</code>. The next page then opens the same
            way. Choosing System deletes it. When you press Drink Me or Eat Me
            in the Text Size controls, the text size is kept the same way, under
            the name <code>{TEXT_SCALE_KEY}</code>. Just Right deletes it. Both
            stay in your browser. Nothing else is stored in your browser. The{' '}
            <dfn>analytics script</dfn>, the code that counts visits, reads one
            local storage setting, named plausible_ignore, which is Plausible's
            own off switch, and writes nothing. Clearing this website's data in
            your browser removes both settings.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Nothing loaded from other websites.</strong> Every file a
            page loads comes from this website: the fonts, the images and the
            analytics script. There are no videos or posts embedded from other
            websites, no third-party tracking tools and no advertising. The
            website's own <dfn>security policy</dfn>, a rule your browser
            enforces, blocks every other website. The one exception: the
            analytics script may send visit counts to Plausible at{' '}
            {PLAUSIBLE_HOST}. Error reports your browser sends to Cloudflare on
            its own are described under Hosting.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No accounts.</strong> There is nothing to sign up for and
            nothing to log in to. What the contact form stores, and the log kept
            of each submission, are described below.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h2>Visit Counts</h2>

        <p>
          I count visits with <a href="https://plausible.io/">Plausible</a>, an
          analytics service that uses no cookies and does not follow you from
          website to website. The script runs from this website, and what it
          sends goes to Plausible at {PLAUSIBLE_HOST}, which stores it on
          servers in the European Union. It is the only place a page here sends
          anything to.
        </p>

        <p>
          I use Plausible's free trial. When the trial ends, Plausible stops
          counting and later deletes what it stored, as{' '}
          <a href={PLAUSIBLE_TRIAL}>its trial terms</a> describe.
        </p>

        <p>For each page you open, the analytics script sends:</p>

        <ul role="list">
          <li>the page's address</li>
          <li>
            the address of the page that linked you here, if your browser shares
            it
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>Both addresses are sent without anything after a ? or #.</p>

        <p>
          When you leave the page or switch away from it, the script also sends
          how far down you scrolled and how long you had the page in view.
        </p>

        <p>When you click a link or a button, it also sends:</p>

        <ul role="list">
          <li>what kind of control it was</li>
          <li>its label</li>
          <li>which part of the page it is in</li>
          <li>for a link, where it goes</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          For an email link the address is left out. Nothing you type is ever
          sent, including what you write in the contact form.
        </p>

        <p>
          Like any server, Plausible receives your IP address and your browser's
          details with each request. Plausible says it uses the IP address to
          work out your country, region and city. It says it uses your browser
          details to work out your browser, operating system and type of device.
          To count each visitor once a day, it turns both, with a random value,
          into an identifier. It deletes the random value every 24 hours. It
          says it does not store the IP address or the browser details
          themselves. How Plausible handles them is covered by{' '}
          <a href={PLAUSIBLE_DATA_POLICY}>Plausible's data policy</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          If your browser sends <dfn>Do Not Track</dfn>, a privacy setting most
          browsers offer, nothing is sent at all. Blocking JavaScript for this
          website stops it too.
        </p>

        <h2>Links Away from Here</h2>

        <p>
          The footer links to my profiles on other websites, and posts may link
          elsewhere. Those are ordinary links: nothing is sent when the page
          loads, only when you follow one. Once you do, that website's own
          policy applies, not mine.
        </p>

        <h2>Hosting</h2>

        <p>
          My website is served by a <dfn>Cloudflare Worker</dfn>, a program that
          runs on Cloudflare's servers. Delivering a page means Cloudflare
          receives the request for it, including your IP address, in the same
          way any web server does. What Cloudflare keeps, and for how long, is
          covered by{' '}
          <a href={CLOUDFLARE_PRIVACY}>Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>, not by
          this one.
        </p>

        <p>
          Requests that run my website's own code are also logged to my
          Cloudflare account, so I can find and fix problems. In practice, that
          means sending the contact form. A log entry holds the request and
          response, which can include your IP address, and your browser and
          approximate location: city, country and network provider. A{' '}
          <dfn>trace</dfn>, a timed record of the same request, holds its timing
          and status and the address requested. It also holds your browser and
          device, approximate location, network provider and the database
          lookups it ran. Logs and traces are deleted automatically after{' '}
          {WORKER_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS} days and are not stored with your message.
          Loading a page in a browser is not logged this way.
        </p>

        <p>
          Cloudflare also asks your browser to report requests to my website
          that fail, a feature called Network Error Logging. Only failures are
          reported, never a successful visit, and only by browsers that support
          it. Your browser sends the report itself, to {NEL_REPORT_HOST}, not to
          my website, and the page's own security policy does not apply to it. A
          report holds the address requested, the type of error and its timing.
          Cloudflare says it stores no IP address or other personal data from
          these reports; its{' '}
          <a href={CLOUDFLARE_NEL}>Network Error Logging documentation</a> says
          how.
        </p>

        <h2>The Contact Form</h2>

        <p>
          The form on <a href="/contact/">the contact page</a> stores the name,
          email address and message you type, so I can reply. Stored with it
          are:
        </p>

        <ul role="list">
          <li>the time it was sent</li>
          <li>the time the emailed copy below went out</li>
          <li>how many times a failed copy has been retried</li>
          <li>a random identifier that only tells one message from another</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          No IP address, browser or page you came from is stored with it. It is
          stored in a database restricted to the European Union. A copy is
          emailed to my inbox, which Google hosts, so I can read and answer it.
        </p>

        <p>
          To stop the form being used for bulk submissions, I limit submissions
          from one network address to about five a minute. For an address in the
          newer format,{' '}
          <Abbr short="IPv6" long="Internet Protocol version 6" after="," /> the
          limit covers its whole /64 network. That is the block a single
          connection is normally given. The limit is counted by Cloudflare's{' '}
          <dfn>rate limiter</dfn>, the service that enforces it. It counts
          against a <Abbr short="SHA-256" long="Secure Hash Algorithm 256" />{' '}
          hash, a one-way fingerprint. The hash is made from the current date
          and your address in the older format,{' '}
          <Abbr short="IPv4" long="Internet Protocol version 4" after="," /> or
          your IPv6 /64. My website writes the hash nowhere else: it is not
          stored with your message or linked to it. A hash of an address is not
          anonymous. The date is no secret, so anyone holding the hash of an
          IPv4 address could recover the address by hashing every possible one.
        </p>

        <p>
          A daily job deletes messages from the database once they are{' '}
          {RETENTION_DAYS} days old. A run that fails is not retried, so a
          message can outlast {RETENTION_DAYS} days until the next day's run
          succeeds. The database can be restored to any moment in the last{' '}
          {D1_RESTORE_WINDOW_DAYS} days. A deleted message can therefore still
          be brought back for {D1_RESTORE_WINDOW_DAYS} days after it is deleted.
          I keep the emailed copy like any other email, for as long as I need it
          to reply. To have both removed sooner, write to{' '}
          <a href={CONTACT_MAILTO}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and say so; you do not
          need to give a reason.
        </p>

        <h2>Email</h2>

        <p>
          If you write to <a href={CONTACT_MAILTO}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, I keep
          your message so I can reply. I do not add it to any list or pass it
          on.
        </p>

        <h2>Questions</h2>

        <p>
          Ask me at <a href={CONTACT_MAILTO}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
